  "text": "Question nine. How does rate limiting work? It caps a client's requests per window, to protect the system and keep it fair. Say ten new links a minute, and one client sends ten at fifty-nine seconds, then ten at sixty-one. A fixed-window counter resets on the minute, so all twenty get through: twice the limit, for one cheap counter. A sliding-window counter weighs in the minute before and refuses the second ten, though it's an estimate that assumes requests were evenly spread. A token bucket holds ten tokens and refills one every six seconds. Each request spends one, so a client that's been quiet can burst ten at once, and an empty bucket answers HTTP four twenty-nine, too many requests. Here the first burst empties it, and the second is refused. A leaky bucket queues requests and lets them out at a constant rate, one every six seconds, so the servers behind it see a smooth stream, and a burst waits its turn. The follow-up is where the count lives. With four app servers, each keeping its own bucket, round-robin hands one client four buckets: four times the limit. The count belongs in a store they all share.",
